WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.972
In the first guided practice, you will write two read-only queries.

00:00:03.972 --> 00:00:09.562
For Task A, return the requested patient columns and sort from oldest to youngest.

00:00:09.562 --> 00:00:14.667
Before writing the SQL, identify the selected columns and the sort direction.

00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:22.227
For Task B, keep only active patients who meet the age threshold, again sorted from oldest to youngest.

00:00:22.227 --> 00:00:24.917
Write the query in the browser and run it.

00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:28.897
Before checking the provided guidance, inspect your own result.

00:00:28.897 --> 00:00:34.257
For the reset dataset, think about which patient should appear first in the all-patient query and which

00:00:34.257 --> 00:00:38.142
patients should remain after the active-and-age filter.

00:00:38.142 --> 00:00:43.842
This is a good place to practice debugging because SELECT does not change the stored rows.

00:00:43.842 --> 00:00:47.297
Change one clause at a time and observe the result.

00:00:47.297 --> 00:00:50.922
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:50.922 --> 00:00:55.987
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:55.987 --> 00:00:59.522
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:59.522 --> 00:01:03.877
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:03.877 --> 00:01:10.962
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:10.962 --> 00:01:17.327
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:17.327 --> 00:01:19.617
not only whether the statement executes.
